# States, requests and errors

A request, a stored setting and a physical result are three different facts. Never write one as if it were another.

<h2 id="report-states">Report states</h2>

<h3 id="wg-031">Separate state from action</h3>

Show what a device last reported apart from what a button will ask it to do.

<%= example('wg-state-action') %>

<h3 id="wg-033">Keep unavailable, unknown, stale and offline apart</h3>

Use the state Home Assistant gives, and show the time with any old reading. None of these means zero, off or broken.

| State | What it means | How to write it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unavailable | Home Assistant cannot get the entity’s state now, for example because the integration cannot reach the device. | Hall sensor unavailable since 18:42. |
| Unknown | The entity exists but Home Assistant has no usable value for it, for example just after Home Assistant starts. | Temperature unknown. No value reported yet. |
| An old reading | The last value received. Nothing newer has arrived. | Last reported 19.4°C at 09:15. |
| Offline | The connection is known to be lost, for example a connectivity sensor reports Disconnected. Without that evidence, write unavailable. | Hub offline since 07:10. |

Read the full meanings of [unavailable](../style-guides/technical-a-to-z-u.html#ht-state-records-unavailable) and [unknown](../style-guides/technical-a-to-z-u.html#ht-state-records-unknown) in the technical A to Z.

<h3 id="wg-038">Make empty and missing states clear</h3>

Say which of three things happened: nothing matches, nothing exists yet, or something could not load.

Write “No devices match these filters”, “No devices have been added yet” or “Devices could not be loaded”, never “No data” for all three.

<h2 id="report-requests-and-actions">Report requests and actions</h2>

<h3 id="wg-032">Say a request was sent, not that it worked</h3>

Selecting a control sends a request. Only a new state from the device shows whether it worked.

Not: Front door locked.

Instead: Lock request sent. Waiting for the front door lock to report its state.

<h3 id="wg-044">Explain pending and uncertain outcomes</h3>

When no new state arrives, say the request may still complete. If sending it again could repeat the action, warn people before they try.

Not: Garage door failed. Try again.

Instead: The garage door has not reported a new state. It may still be moving. Check the door before you send another request.

<h3 id="wg-035">Keep saved, enabled and running apart</h3>

An automation can be saved, turned on so it starts by itself, and running now. Say which of these changed. Saving a change does not start the automation, and turning it off does not change what was saved.

<%= example('wg-automation-status') %>

<h3 id="wg-034">Explain what the control can and cannot do</h3>

Show only the actions a device supports. When one is missing or cannot be used, say why and who can change that.

For example: “The washing machine reports that remote start is off, so Home Assistant cannot start a cycle. Turn on remote start on the machine.”

<h3 id="wg-036">Say what Cancel, Stop and Delete do</h3>

Name the thing, and say what stops, what carries on and what is lost. Say “you can undo this” only when it can be undone.

Not: Are you sure?

Instead: Stop the garden watering now? Tomorrow’s 06:00 watering will still run.

<h2 id="report-problems">Report problems</h2>

<h3 id="wg-040">Give a recovery that matches the cause</h3>

Name the cause you know, and offer one step that fits it. Do not ask people to fix something that may not be wrong.

Not: Failed. Restart your router.

Instead: The heating hub did not reply. Wait a minute, then save the schedule again.

<h3 id="wg-041">Tell a mistake from a fault</h3>

If the person can fix it, say exactly what to change. If the system failed, say so.

Not: Invalid input.

Instead: Enter a temperature between 5°C and 30°C.

<h3 id="wg-042">Choose urgency from evidence</h3>

Base urgency on what a sensor or record shows, and [keep alarm wording for real alarms](right-tone.html#wg-046). A date in a record that needs checking is a reminder, not an emergency. Say why the reminder appears and what to do next.

<%= example('wg-warning-context') %>

Put a warning before the step it affects. Say what the hazard is, what could happen and what to do. Use “Danger”, “Warning” and “Caution” only for risks to people, not for risks to a device.

Match the message to how long it needs to stay:

- a toast disappears after a few seconds, so never put a fix people need in one
- an alert stays until the problem is gone

A repair comes from Home Assistant or an integration, not from you. If one covers the problem, point people to the repair rather than repeating its steps.

<h3 id="wg-043">Stay inside what the system can know</h3>

Do not give safety, medical or legal advice that the system cannot back up. Say what the sensors reported, and what that does not prove.

Not: Your home is safe.

Instead: No smoke alarm has reported smoke in the last hour. This does not mean the house is safe.
